WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.141
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online B two, Book two.

2
00:00:05.441 --> 00:00:09.292
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.592 --> 00:00:15.425
Unit twenty-one: Scientific Thinking. Today you will describe facts and results with the zero conditional.

4
00:00:15.725 --> 00:00:18.192
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.133 --> 00:00:22.598
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.450 --> 00:00:24.718
Hypothesis.

7
00:00:25.717 --> 00:00:28.877
Hypothesis means an idea you test with an experiment.

8
00:00:29.483 --> 00:00:32.795
My hypothesis is that plants grow faster with music.

9
00:00:33.383 --> 00:00:36.059
Your turn. Say: Hypothesis.

10
00:00:38.900 --> 00:00:40.319
Hypothesis.

11
00:00:41.083 --> 00:00:42.201
Experiment.

12
00:00:43.217 --> 00:00:45.832
Experiment means a test to find out something.

13
00:00:46.417 --> 00:00:48.695
We did an experiment with ice and salt.

14
00:00:49.283 --> 00:00:51.924
Your turn. Say: Experiment.

15
00:00:54.767 --> 00:00:55.826
Experiment.

16
00:00:56.583 --> 00:00:57.666
Evidence.

17
00:00:58.650 --> 00:01:01.416
Evidence means facts that show something is true.

18
00:01:02.017 --> 00:01:04.272
We need evidence, not opinions.

19
00:01:04.883 --> 00:01:07.245
Your turn. Say: Evidence.

20
00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:11.136
Evidence.

21
00:01:11.883 --> 00:01:12.629
Data.

22
00:01:13.617 --> 00:01:16.394
Data means facts and numbers collected for study.

23
00:01:16.983 --> 00:01:19.064
We recorded the data in a table.

24
00:01:19.650 --> 00:01:22.511
Your turn. Say: Data.

25
00:01:25.367 --> 00:01:26.194
Data.

26
00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:27.905
Conclusion.

27
00:01:28.917 --> 00:01:31.822
Conclusion means what you decide after the test.

28
00:01:32.417 --> 00:01:35.020
Our conclusion was that salt melts ice.

29
00:01:35.617 --> 00:01:38.594
Your turn. Say: Conclusion.

30
00:01:41.433 --> 00:01:42.527
Conclusion.

31
00:01:43.283 --> 00:01:44.273
Variable.

32
00:01:45.283 --> 00:01:48.119
Variable means something that can change in an experiment.

33
00:01:48.717 --> 00:01:50.914
Temperature is the variable we changed.

34
00:01:51.517 --> 00:01:54.215
Your turn. Say: Variable.

35
00:01:57.067 --> 00:01:58.137
Variable.

36
00:01:58.883 --> 00:01:59.745
Test.

37
00:02:00.750 --> 00:02:03.493
Test means to try something to see if it works.

38
00:02:04.083 --> 00:02:05.700
Let's test the idea.

39
00:02:06.283 --> 00:02:08.576
Your turn. Say: Test.

40
00:02:11.433 --> 00:02:12.376
Test.

41
00:02:13.117 --> 00:02:14.083
Question.

42
00:02:15.083 --> 00:02:17.048
Question means what you want to find out.

43
00:02:17.650 --> 00:02:20.230
Every experiment starts with a question.

44
00:02:20.817 --> 00:02:23.202
Your turn. Say: Question.

45
00:02:26.067 --> 00:02:26.894
Question.

46
00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:31.339
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:32.183 --> 00:02:34.868
Visal, what is scientific thinking?

48
00:02:35.983 --> 00:02:42.780
It is testing ideas with evidence. If you heat water to 100°C, it boils.

49
00:02:43.883 --> 00:02:46.382
How do scientists test a hypothesis?

50
00:02:47.483 --> 00:02:50.644
They do an experiment and change one variable.

51
00:02:51.750 --> 00:02:52.589
Then what?

52
00:02:53.683 --> 00:02:57.355
If the data supports the idea, they write a conclusion.

53
00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:00.024
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:00.883 --> 00:03:03.370
Grammar focus: Zero conditional.

55
00:03:03.670 --> 00:03:04.811
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:05.517 --> 00:03:09.211
If you heat water to 100°C, it boils.

57
00:03:10.117 --> 00:03:12.604
If you mix blue and yellow, you get green.

58
00:03:13.517 --> 00:03:15.667
If plants don't get light, they die.

59
00:03:16.633 --> 00:03:22.339
Use the zero conditional for facts and things that always happen. Both parts use the present simple.

60
00:03:23.083 --> 00:03:28.243
Be careful! Don't say: If you heat water to 100 degrees, it will boiling.

61
00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:34.359
Say: If you heat water to 100 degrees, it boils.

62
00:03:34.659 --> 00:03:38.516
The zero conditional uses the present simple in both parts.

63
00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:42.161
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:03:42.967 --> 00:03:44.490
Think Like a Scientist.

65
00:03:44.790 --> 00:03:47.208
Scientific thinking starts with a question.

66
00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:51.047
Next, you make a hypothesis and plan an experiment.

67
00:03:52.033 --> 00:03:54.335
You change one variable and collect data.

68
00:03:55.333 --> 00:03:58.889
If the evidence supports your idea, you write a conclusion.

69
00:03:59.900 --> 00:04:02.921
If you think like a scientist, you make better decisions.

70
00:04:04.033 --> 00:04:07.931
Part five: quiz. Three questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:08.783 --> 00:04:10.841
If you heat ice, it blank.

72
00:04:11.141 --> 00:04:14.469
A, melts. B, will melting. C, melted.

73
00:04:18.033 --> 00:04:19.650
The answer is A: melts.

74
00:04:20.783 --> 00:04:23.340
An idea you test with an experiment is a blank.

75
00:04:23.640 --> 00:04:27.862
A, hypothesis. B, conclusion. C, variable.

76
00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:33.667
The answer is A: hypothesis.

77
00:04:34.817 --> 00:04:37.432
Facts and numbers collected for study are blank.

78
00:04:37.732 --> 00:04:41.083
A, data. B, questions. C, tests.

79
00:04:44.633 --> 00:04:46.203
The answer is A: data.

80
00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:50.459
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

81
00:04:51.317 --> 00:04:54.199
How can scientific thinking help in daily life?

82
00:05:02.983 --> 00:05:04.553
Now listen to Visal.

83
00:05:04.853 --> 00:05:15.946
It helps us check ideas before we believe them. For example, if I water my plants too much, they die, so I test how much water they really need.

84
00:05:16.246 --> 00:05:17.770
Excellent answer, Visal!

85
00:05:18.683 --> 00:05:22.857
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and zero conditional.

86
00:05:23.157 --> 00:05:26.970
Today's value is curiosity. Ask questions and find out.

87
00:05:27.270 --> 00:05:30.085
See you in Unit twenty-two. Goodbye!

88
00:05:30.385 --> 00:05:33.037
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

